  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1136 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOvernight\n\nLike last night, the combination of clear skies, light winds, and\ndry dew points has resulted in strong radiative cooling this \nevening. The current temperatures are already approaching the \nforecast low temperatures for overnight. Due to this, lowered \nthe overnight low temperatures by using a 50-50 blend of the NBM\nand the NBM 10th percentile for most areas. In central \nWisconsin, went 100 percent NBM 10th percentile. This resulted \nin low temperatures ranging from the mid-30s to mid-40s.\n\nWith a deep layer of light winds up 8K feet overnight, we should\nsee valley fog develop overnight. There are already signs of it\ndeveloping on our office web cam. This valley fog should\ngradually dissipate between 22.15z and 22.16z.\n\nToday through Saturday\n\nThrough Saturday, a strong 500 mb ridge will remain in control \nof the area's weather. This high will provide dry weather and \nunseasonably warm temperatures. With the NBM running on the cool\nside much of last week and since Sunday, used the NBM 90th \npercentile to raise the high temperatures by 2 to 5 degrees from\ntoday through Saturday. This resulted in high temperatures \nranging from the upper 60s to mid-70s through Saturday. \n\nWith the NBM being a bit too warm on low temperatures for the \npast 3 nights, used a 50-50 blend of the NBM and NBM 10th \npercentile to lower them for tonight. This resulted in low \ntemperatures tonight ranging from the mid-40s to mid-50s. On \nFriday and Saturday nights, the low temperatures will range \nfrom the upper 40s to mid-50s.\n\nSunday into Wednesday\n\nFrom Sunday into Monday, a trough of low pressure will move east\nacross Manitoba and Ontario. This will drag a cold front slowly \nsoutheast into the forecast area. There remains plenty of\nuncertainty on how far south this front will get. Some keep it\nmainly to our north. Others drop this front south as Interstate\n80. This result in much uncertainty on temperatures and where \nthe heaviest rain will occur. It still looks like this front \nwill be capable of producing rain amounts up to a half-inch\nwherever it ends up.\n\nFrom Monday night into Wednesday, this front will likely meander\nacross the region. This will impact both temperatures and\nprecipitation probabilities.",
